#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebf18ee-af52-58eb-9db4-f851658ecd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253931} {:answer/id #uuid "1f068fc2-f6b1-5e9e-af2b-751b98bcdc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253936} {:answer/id #uuid "49f209c9-1626-5df5-aefb-bdf07e8a5715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254305} {:answer/id #uuid "de5cdbb3-0dd8-544a-8444-7a6932630c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=1-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255642} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e425f-e29f-5937-be75-0ebb5ff34e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35e425f-e29f-5937-be75-0ebb5ff34e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683} :answer-field/id #uuid "877a8eaf-76c0-5b50-a12d-6f6b23536812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-122170" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a8/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5b2eeb-074d-51bf-9b3f-10803e274c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253770} {:answer/id #uuid "56351265-a52c-5206-885d-fa5dfeb276eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254428} {:answer/id #uuid "afa87942-4e3f-5a55-ae5f-5d22dd94cb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255214} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b91cfd-f727-572e-865c-a94f7aba262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255559} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bf1029-4403-54dd-a55a-c9e8275e658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bf1029-4403-54dd-a55a-c9e8275e658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d4fe-7d5a-50cb-99f3-481a645a95f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2aa7557-417c-5f06-bca6-b63a94c74ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/47/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08989d71-d44c-554a-bce5-f49fba344fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253732} {:answer/id #uuid "485c6662-97ec-5254-8bb7-1d15dbc744b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254291} {:answer/id #uuid "d23b0337-7b09-5e9a-8cfa-fce6700a9517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} {:answer/id #uuid "eac5aa36-49a3-5648-998f-1f48c9122a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255726} {:answer/id #uuid "edac6096-b0c5-5959-8d95-58a0e84227a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23b0337-7b09-5e9a-8cfa-fce6700a9517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd69269-dfc3-5489-a6f7-c96d7e0ca756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "226fe9ff-a963-5e5f-900b-474a261bf3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122310" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032c1aed-5869-5996-b874-ec60376b4b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64d957-8cab-5c68-a933-ebb3da3c2a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254193} {:answer/id #uuid "484acfee-7a2b-52bf-a78b-d62f355b27df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254288} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5dc97-9ff5-56d3-816d-361c1ad73349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255472} {:answer/id #uuid "d91174a7-6095-546d-814c-2cd0e4c8e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032c1aed-5869-5996-b874-ec60376b4b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} :answer-field/id #uuid "0641e0bb-3057-51af-9319-471e1e0b8f5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a56f0b7-8350-56d0-8d3b-b2d932d38f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122343" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/0d/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8658c0bb-cfd4-5142-bbcb-e4097359b029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254860} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc47ff9-abd8-5a14-858a-de531e040383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255056} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ad6d9c-592b-5e0c-8122-d62c500c3d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255273} {:answer/id #uuid "b9989186-dace-584b-b203-97a637274263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0cc1f7-7a63-5b1c-a0d5-346590852e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9989186-dace-584b-b203-97a637274263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd66982-5b26-55ef-acff-14d539285859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d544f0e-e224-5a8f-b2d9-7d23deb9a689" :question/math-academy-id "q-122347" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/3c/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}]}
